{{anchor|Chart notes}}'''Notes:'''
#{{note|a}}Primarily used in classical orthography; after the reform used word-initially and in some compound words.
#{{note|b}}Except in {{Lang|hy|ով}} {{IPA|/ɔv/|lang=hy}} 'who' and {{Lang|hy|ովքեր}} {{IPA|/ɔvkʰer/|lang=hy}} 'those (people)' in Eastern Armenian.
#{{note|c}}[[Iranian Armenians]] (who speak a subbranch of Eastern Armenian) pronounce the sound represented by this letter with a retracted tongue body {{IPA|[ɹ̠]}}: post-alveolar rather than alveolar. {{Citation needed|date=January 2019}}
#{{note|d}}In classical orthography, {{Lang|hy|ու}} and {{Lang|hy|և}} are considered a digraph ({{Lang|hy|ո}} + {{Lang|hy|ւ}}) and a ligature ({{Lang|hy|ե}} + {{Lang|hy|ւ}}), respectively. In reformed orthography, they are separate letters of the alphabet: {{Lang|hy|և}} is the 37th letter of the alphabet, and {{Lang|hy|ու}} is the 34th letter, taking the place of {{Lang|hy|ւ}}.
#{{note|e}}In reformed orthography, the letter {{Lang|hy|ւ}} appears only as a component of {{Lang|hy|ու}}. In classical orthography, the letter usually represents {{IPA|/v/}}, except in the digraph {{Lang|hy|իւ}} {{IPA|/ju/}}. The spelling reform in Soviet Armenia replaced {{Lang|hy|իւ}} with the trigraph {{Lang|hy|յու}}.
#{{note|f}}Except in the present tense of 'to be': {{Lang|hy|եմ}} {{IPA|/ɛm/|lang=hy}} 'I am', {{Lang|hy|ես}} {{IPA|/ɛs/|lang=hy}} 'you are (sing.)', {{Lang|hy|ենք}} {{IPA|/ɛnkʰ/|lang=hy}} 'we are', {{Lang|hy|եք}} {{IPA|/ɛkʰ/|lang=hy}} 'you are (pl.)', {{Lang|hy|են}} {{IPA|/ɛn/|lang=hy}} 'they are'.
#{{note|g}}The letter {{Lang|hy|ը}} is generally used only at the start or end of a word, and so the sound {{IPA|/ə/}} is typically unwritten between consonants. One exception is {{Lang|hy|մըն}} {{IPA|/mən/|lang=hy}} (Western Armenian indefinite article, when followed by a word beginning with a vowel), e.g., {{Lang|hy|մէյ մըն ալ}} {{IPA|/mɛj mən ɑl/|lang=hy}} 'one more time'.
#{{note|h}}The ligature {{Lang|hy|և}} has no majuscule form; when capitalized it is written as two letters {{Lang|hy|Եւ}} (classical) or {{Lang|hy|Եվ}} (reformed).
#{{note|i}}By the time this lowercase was included (Armenian alphabet reform of 1922–24 in the Soviet Union) in the alphabet, counting was conducted with Arabic numbers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Армянское письмо |url=http://tapemark.narod.ru/les/045a.html |access-date=2024-02-09 |website=tapemark.narod.ru}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=MELA ConC - Numerals & Dates |url=https://sites.google.com/princeton.edu/mela-conc/cataloging-manuals/armenian/armnum |access-date=2024-02-09 |website=sites.google.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Numbers over 9999 were achieved by putting a line over smaller letters-numerals

